  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What are warp and weft?",
      "a": "The warp is the set of long threads held tight along the loom. The weft is the thread passed across, over and under the warp threads. Together they lock into woven cloth.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the difference between warp and weft?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do the heddles do on a loom?",
      "a": "Heddles are wires or cords with an eye for each warp thread. Lifting one group of heddles raises their warp threads, opening a gap so the weft can pass under some threads and over others.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the shed on a loom?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the shuttle do?",
      "a": "The shuttle holds a bobbin of weft thread and is thrown or pushed through the shed, laying a line of weft across the warp each time.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a flying shuttle?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is the weft beaten with the reed?",
      "a": "The reed pushes each new weft thread firmly against the woven cloth so the threads sit close and even. Beating harder makes a denser, stronger cloth.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the reed on a loom?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do modern looms work so fast?",
      "a": "Modern factory looms have no shuttle: they shoot the weft across with a jet of air or water, or carry it with thin metal grippers, inserting the weft over a thousand times a minute under computer control.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an air-jet loom?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "John Kay's flying shuttle of 1733 let one weaver make wide cloth much faster.",
    "The Jacquard loom of 1804 used punched cards to control patterns, an idea that inspired early computers."
  ],
